{#- The promote step, which used to be a terminal command — so the review it
   was supposed to provide only existed for whoever had the Pi in front of them.

   THIS PAGE IS MOSTLY A LIST, AND THAT IS THE POINT. Publishing carries every
   code change on the working branch, not just the sentence somebody edited.
   A button labelled "Publish" with no list behind it is a button that ships
   two consoles and seven migrations because somebody fixed a typo. -#}

<p class="rolegate" id="pNotConfigured" hidden></p>

<div class="adm-publish" id="pRoot" hidden>

  <div class="p-state" id="pState"></div>

  {#- Named separately from the file list, and above the button. A migration in
     a release is not one more changed file — it is something that must be run
     against the production database BEFORE this deploys, or the live site
     starts throwing on a table that is not there. -#}
  <div class="p-migrations" id="pMigrations" hidden></div>

  <div class="p-cols">
    <section class="p-col">
      <h3 data-i18n="pub.commits">Commits</h3>
      <div class="p-commits" id="pCommits"></div>
    </section>
    <section class="p-col">
      <h3 data-i18n="pub.files">Files</h3>
      <div class="p-files" id="pFiles"></div>
    </section>
  </div>

  {#- Shown only when the live branch holds commits the working branch has not
     got. That happens because the production console's own content editor
     commits straight to the live branch — so a typo fixed on the live site is
     a change `dev` does not know about until this is pressed. -#}
  <div class="p-drift" id="pDrift" hidden></div>

</div>

<div class="ms-savebar" id="pBar" role="status" hidden>
  <span class="ms-dirty-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <span id="pBarCount"></span>
  <span class="ms-savebar-note" id="pBarNote"></span>
  <button type="button" class="ghost-btn" id="pRefresh" data-i18n="pub.refresh">Refresh</button>
  <button type="button" class="solid-btn" id="pPublish" data-i18n="pub.publish">Publish to live</button>
</div>
